Architecture
============

Shape
-----

A single page React application. No server rendering, no data framework, no
global store. State is local to each page; shared state is limited to
authentication.

	main.tsx
	  imports index.css
	  renders <App />

	App.tsx
	  <BrowserRouter>
	    <AuthProvider>
	      /login                 public
	      <AdminLayout>          everything else
	        /app                 Overview
	        /app/challenges      Challenges
	        /app/challenges/new  CreateChallenge
	        /app/challenge/:id   ChallengeDetail
	        /app/validations     Validations
	        /app/leaderboards    Leaderboards
	        /app/quests          Quests
	      *                      redirect to /app


The authenticated shell
-----------------------

AdminLayout is the gate and the chrome in one component. It:

* reads the auth context and redirects to /login when there is no user,
* opens the Socket.IO connection only after a user exists,
* fetches the pending validation count for the sidebar badge and refreshes it
  on validation:submitted and validation:resolved,
* renders the sidebar and an <Outlet /> for the current page,
* tears the socket down on sign out.

The reason the socket and the first API call live here rather than at the root
is ordering. An earlier version fetched on first paint, before a token
existed, and every request 401'd against the login screen. Nothing that
requires a token should mount above AdminLayout.


Authentication
--------------

context/AuthContext.tsx holds the current admin and exposes login, logout and
isLoading. Tokens live in localStorage behind tokenStorage in api/client.ts;
no component reads localStorage directly.

The axios response interceptor handles expiry. On a 401 it attempts a single
refresh, queues any concurrent requests behind that refresh, and replays them.
If the refresh call itself fails it clears the tokens and dispatches a window
event auth:logout, which AuthContext listens for and turns into a redirect.
This is why a failed refresh does not recurse.


Data flow
---------

A page owns its own data:

	useEffect on mount   -> call src/api/<domain>/<x>Api.ts
	                     -> set local state
	socket event         -> call the same loader again
	user action          -> call a mutating api function, then reload

There is no cache and no optimistic update. Reloading after a mutation is
deliberate: the server is the only source of truth for XP, standings and
validation state, and every one of those can be changed by another admin or by
a student while the page is open.

Realtime subscriptions per page are listed in docs/pages.md and, from the
server side, in the backend's docs/realtime.md.


Directory rules
---------------

src/api/
	One folder per domain. An Api.ts file with the calls, a Types.ts file
	with the response shapes. api/client.ts is the shared axios instance and
	token storage. api/socket.ts owns the single socket connection.

src/pages/
	One file per route. A page may hold layout, local state and effects. If a
	block of markup is used by two pages it moves to components/.

src/components/
	Presentational and shared. Components take props and do not fetch, with
	the deliberate exception of nothing at present. Keep it that way.

src/context/
	Cross-cutting state only. Today that is authentication.


Public assets
-------------

public/avatars/faculty1..faculty9/1..5.png and public/facultyBadges/1..9.png
are the real art shared with the mobile app. Vite serves public/ at the web
root, so components reference them as /avatars/faculty3/4.png. They are
copies, not a shared package; if the mobile art is updated these must be
updated too.